00:30:42.985 --> 00:30:44.505
interesting things in your life relative

00:30:44.505 --> 00:30:45.665
to if there's no entrepreneurs

00:30:45.665 --> 00:30:47.105
and we're all just wearing Amazon basics.

00:30:48.955 --> 00:30:50.725
- What I love is, you know, as you go off

00:30:50.725 --> 00:30:52.585
and add more value than you're taking

00:30:52.585 --> 00:30:56.225
and you create success stories
in different communities,

00:30:56.225 --> 00:30:59.565
people can learn from someone
like you in an interview like

00:30:59.565 --> 00:31:00.785
this and get inspired,

00:31:00.785 --> 00:31:04.245
but you're creating little
mini ambassadors all

00:31:04.245 --> 00:31:06.405
around the world, not just for Shopify,

00:31:06.405 --> 00:31:08.925
but for entrepreneurship
that yeah, maybe they can,

00:31:08.925 --> 00:31:10.965
they maybe they can't connect
to me, they can't connect

00:31:10.965 --> 00:31:12.805
to you, but they can
connect to their friend

00:31:12.805 --> 00:31:14.085
who started a Shopify store

00:31:14.085 --> 00:31:16.645
and now they quit their
job and doing it full time.

00:31:16.645 --> 00:31:18.435
It's like, huh, maybe I can do it too.

00:31:18.435 --> 00:31:20.945
Like you're creating this little system

00:31:20.945 --> 00:31:23.005
of ambassadors out there in the world,

00:31:23.005 --> 00:31:24.245
encouraging entrepreneurship, which I love

00:31:25.145 --> 00:31:27.425
the whole small business
versus entrepreneur.

00:31:27.425 --> 00:31:29.785
Do you think, do you think
small business as a term is,

00:31:29.785 --> 00:31:32.205
is contributes to limited
internal thinking?

00:31:34.705 --> 00:31:36.055
- I don't have a better version of that.

00:31:36.055 --> 00:31:38.905
I'll just use my own personal
situation with my wife.

00:31:42.065 --> 00:31:44.355
Lindsay just doesn't like
the term entrepreneur

00:31:44.355 --> 00:31:46.495
because it's the term
that I used ad nauseum.

00:31:46.495 --> 00:31:47.715
I'm always talking about entrepreneurship.

00:31:47.715 --> 00:31:48.895
And she's like, no, no, that's you.

00:31:48.895 --> 00:31:51.255
I'm like small business ice cream person.

00:31:51.255 --> 00:31:54.075
And I, I, I, I think nomenclature,

00:31:54.075 --> 00:31:56.855
I think naming things
really, really matter.

00:31:56.855 --> 00:32:01.675
And so I think, you know,

00:32:01.675 --> 00:32:03.335
Shopify started as a small business

00:32:03.335 --> 00:32:05.055
and it is now, I think the second

00:32:05.055 --> 00:32:06.075
or something like that, one

00:32:06.075 --> 00:32:07.195
of the largest companies in the country.

00:32:08.335 --> 00:32:10.115
At some point we probably,
I think we were the largest

00:32:10.115 --> 00:32:11.855
company in the country last year

00:32:11.855 --> 00:32:14.155
by market cap ahead of
the Royal Bank. Does

00:32:14.155 --> 00:32:15.155
- That sound crazy?

00:32:15.155 --> 00:32:16.795
- Like I, I never, by that
sounds totally crazy amazing,

00:32:16.795 --> 00:32:18.475
bigger than it's, I mean, Royal Bank,

00:32:18.475 --> 00:32:19.715
it's, it's an, it's an amazing thing.

00:32:19.715 --> 00:32:21.995
I think now we're still the
second or third largest c you

00:32:21.995 --> 00:32:23.995
know, company in, in,
in all of Canada, which,

00:32:23.995 --> 00:32:25.115
which is an amazing thing

00:32:25.115 --> 00:32:26.535
from an, from an entrepreneur perspective.

00:32:26.535 --> 00:32:28.635
But I'd never, I, I never
felt limited by the fact

00:32:28.635 --> 00:32:30.055
that Shopify was a small business

00:32:30.055 --> 00:32:32.255
or I, we never referred
shop as a small business.

00:32:32.255 --> 00:32:34.455
It was just Shopify was a vehicle

00:32:34.455 --> 00:32:37.075
to achieve a goal, achieve a mission.

00:32:37.075 --> 00:32:39.105
I think that's a much better
way to think about it.

00:32:39.105 --> 00:32:43.815
So if I had to roll back, not that I need

00:32:43.815 --> 00:32:45.895
to give Lindsay any advice,
nor would she take the advice

00:32:45.895 --> 00:32:48.395
from me, but, but Lindsay

00:32:48.395 --> 00:32:50.775
and I were walking when we
first had our, our first child,

00:32:50.775 --> 00:32:52.875
Bailey, around our
community here in Ottawa,

00:32:52.875 --> 00:32:54.135
and we wanted to go for an ice cream.

00:32:54.135 --> 00:32:57.555
There wasn't an ice
cream. And Lindsay started

00:32:57.555 --> 00:33:00.415
to take some notes and test that ice cream

00:33:00.415 --> 00:33:02.235
and learn about ice cream
and learn about like the

00:33:02.235 --> 00:33:03.295
air content in ice cream.

00:33:03.295 --> 00:33:06.655
Basically ice cream quality
is, is effectively a derivative

00:33:06.655 --> 00:33:07.875
of how much air is in the ice cream.

00:33:07.875 --> 00:33:10.595
So like a lot of air
is like Baskin Robbins

00:33:10.595 --> 00:33:13.415
and a little bit of air
is like Haagen does,

00:33:13.415 --> 00:33:14.375
it's just quality of ice cream

00:33:14.375 --> 00:33:15.675
changes based on how much air.

00:33:15.675 --> 00:33:17.955
So she went like deep into it,

00:33:17.955 --> 00:33:19.215
and then she set up Sunday school

00:33:19.215 --> 00:33:21.115
and started selling ice
cream to the community.

00:33:21.115 --> 00:33:22.575
You could articulate as a small business

00:33:22.575 --> 00:33:24.895
or an entrepreneur, a venture,
but ultimately all she was

00:33:24.895 --> 00:33:27.115
trying to do is solve a problem.

00:33:27.115 --> 00:33:30.255
The problem was, our community
here, Beachwood, which is

00:33:30.255 --> 00:33:30.935
where we live in Ottawa,

00:33:30.935 --> 00:33:32.215
Beachwood, did not have ice cream shop.

00:33:32.215 --> 00:33:34.575
She wanted Beachwood to
have an ice cream shop

00:33:34.575 --> 00:33:36.315
because people she thought
would like ice cream.

00:33:36.315 --> 00:33:38.775
She solved the problem. I
think we should go in if,

00:33:38.775 --> 00:33:41.255
if we sort of, you know, think
about this is like solving

00:33:41.255 --> 00:33:44.595
problems as opposed to
putting monikers or titles or,

00:33:44.595 --> 00:33:47.415
or any type of branding on it,
I think it's a better thing

00:33:47.415 --> 00:33:49.375
to do it, but honestly, I, I don't know if

00:33:49.375 --> 00:33:52.095
that's necessarily what's
holding back an explosion.

00:33:52.095 --> 00:33:54.295
Entrepreneurship. I think
what's holding back an explosion

00:33:54.295 --> 00:33:58.535
of entrepreneur
entrepreneurship is we as a,

00:33:58.535 --> 00:34:01.575
as a society generally
don't embrace failure

00:34:01.575 --> 00:34:03.135
the way we probably should.

00:34:03.135 --> 00:34:05.195
And I think it's even more
prevalent here in Canada.

00:34:05.195 --> 00:34:07.955
I, you know, I was born in
Montreal, I live in Canada,

00:34:07.955 --> 00:34:10.055
probably live in Canada
the rest of my life.

00:34:10.055 --> 00:34:12.595
But I I, I grew up, you know,
I went to high school in,

00:34:12.595 --> 00:34:14.915
in south Florida, and I think in, in, in,

00:34:14.915 --> 00:34:16.155
I think Canada actually tends

00:34:16.155 --> 00:34:18.575
to be even more risk averse
than, than most countries,

00:34:18.575 --> 00:34:21.635
at least relative to the
US in terms of failure.

00:34:22.935 --> 00:34:24.355
We tend to, you know, there's

00:34:24.355 --> 00:34:26.475
that tall poppy syndrome complex

00:34:26.475 --> 00:34:29.595
that gets brought up
fairly often, I think,

00:34:29.595 --> 00:34:30.855
I think you can get around that,

00:34:30.855 --> 00:34:32.125
but I think it's, it's certainly present,

00:34:32.125 --> 00:34:35.675
which is also why I like doing
great shows like yours here,

00:34:35.675 --> 00:34:39.735
where I can talk more about
Shopify, I can talk more about,

00:34:39.735 --> 00:34:41.355
you know, my life and my ambitions

00:34:41.355 --> 00:34:42.595
and where I think Shopify can go

00:34:42.595 --> 00:34:44.515
and why I think everyone
in the world should be an

00:34:44.515 --> 00:34:45.835
entrepreneur at some point, or at least

00:34:45.835 --> 00:34:46.875
a lot more people should be entrepreneurs.

00:34:46.875 --> 00:34:51.075
Because I think that those
of us that have had any type

00:34:51.075 --> 00:34:52.795
of success, those of us, that, those of us

00:34:52.795 --> 00:34:56.155
that have any platform
whatsoever, we have to use

00:34:56.155 --> 00:34:58.935
that platform to, to change something.

00:34:58.935 --> 00:35:03.475
And you know, I I've,
years ago I used to go like

00:35:03.475 --> 00:35:04.775
to like Startup Fest store

00:35:04.775 --> 00:35:06.475
and at a big Canadian tech conferences,

00:35:06.475 --> 00:35:08.635
and I get asked, you know,
what would I, what would I do

00:35:08.635 --> 00:35:10.595
to fix up with the Canadian tech world?

00:35:10.595 --> 00:35:12.715
And I would often talk about
the fact that, you know,

00:35:12.715 --> 00:35:15.175
I want to see less acquirers
and more acquirers.

00:35:15.175 --> 00:35:16.795
And what I meant by that
was I don't, not necessarily

00:35:16.795 --> 00:35:18.435
that I wanted to see an increase in m

00:35:18.435 --> 00:35:21.665
and a activity, I don't like that's fine.

00:35:21.665 --> 00:35:25.395
What I meant though is that I
often would get asked about a

00:35:25.395 --> 00:35:28.035
company in Canada, you know, Hey, what,

00:35:28.035 --> 00:35:30.235
what should we do if we
want to get acquired by Google?

00:35:31.175 --> 00:35:33.915
And I always thought of, I always sort

00:35:33.915 --> 00:35:36.635
of thought it'd be a fun thought
experiment to, to say back

00:35:36.635 --> 00:35:38.045
to them, well, have you thought about

00:35:38.045 --> 00:35:39.755
acquiring Google yourself?

00:35:39.755 --> 00:35:42.005
Like you acquiring Google?
Was Google acquiring you?

00:35:42.005 --> 00:35:43.765
And, and of course that
would like, you know,

00:35:43.765 --> 00:35:44.725
it would break their brains and

00:35:44.725 --> 00:35:45.365
be like, whatcha talking about?

00:35:45.365 --> 00:35:46.915
It was like, well, like,
you should think that way.

00:35:46.915 --> 00:35:49.065
Like there is a chance and,

00:35:49.065 --> 00:35:51.545
and a way for you to be as big as that.

00:35:51.545 --> 00:35:55.585
And Shopify was, our ambitions
were always really big.

00:35:55.585 --> 00:35:58.765
The reason we we went
public in 2015 was we want

00:35:58.765 --> 00:36:01.945
to be a hundred year independent,
very large scale company.

00:36:01.945 --> 00:36:02.845
We thought that was the best

00:36:02.845 --> 00:36:04.185
way for our, achieve our mission.

00:36:04.185 --> 00:36:06.765
And to do that, the IPO
was a part of that journey.

00:36:06.765 --> 00:36:08.385
And so not only did we go public,

00:36:08.385 --> 00:36:11.465
but we were very vocal
about our o our IPO process,

00:36:11.465 --> 00:36:13.485
why we were going public, why
we thought it was a good idea

00:36:13.485 --> 00:36:14.525
to go public, so on

00:36:14.525 --> 00:36:16.965
and so forth. I think that stuff matters.

00:36:16.965 --> 00:36:19.685
- I love it, man. I, I'm, I'm
curious like how to do it.

00:36:19.685 --> 00:36:22.505
I remember somebody asked me
if I had an unlimited budget

00:36:22.505 --> 00:36:24.795
to get my YouTube channel
set up, what should I do?

00:36:24.795 --> 00:36:26.125
It's like, okay, you got unlimited budget,

00:36:26.125 --> 00:36:27.625
you should hire like these five people

00:36:27.625 --> 00:36:29.985
and start your, start your
channel and start to grow.

00:36:29.985 --> 00:36:32.925
And then I, I sat back later
that night, said, well,

00:36:32.925 --> 00:36:34.125
he said unlimited budget.

00:36:34.125 --> 00:36:37.785
Like he should buy YouTube,
not just hire five people

00:36:37.785 --> 00:36:38.855
and start his channel.

00:36:38.855 --> 00:36:40.725
Right, exactly. So right.

00:36:40.725 --> 00:36:43.505
It's, and like, I it, but
we just don't live there.

00:36:43.505 --> 00:36:44.905
We don't live in that like,

00:36:44.905 --> 00:36:47.305
I'm going to buy YouTube kind of mindset.

00:36:47.305 --> 00:36:52.285
Is there, like, how do you
keep that big thinking front

00:36:52.285 --> 00:36:55.435
of mind as especially the
leader of this company

00:36:55.435 --> 00:36:57.285
that we're going to go
and change the world?

00:36:57.285 --> 00:37:00.205
Like how do you not just
fall back to the norm

00:37:00.205 --> 00:37:01.605
and what everybody else is doing? Yeah,

00:37:02.515 --> 00:37:03.765
- There's a couple hacks to it,

00:37:03.765 --> 00:37:05.365
but I think they're all
sort of per, they're,

00:37:05.365 --> 00:37:07.325
they're all based on your
own personality and, and,

00:37:07.325 --> 00:37:09.165
and, and what works for you.

00:37:09.165 --> 00:37:10.545
I have been really lucky

00:37:10.545 --> 00:37:12.845
and fortunate to surround
myself with a group of people,

00:37:12.845 --> 00:37:17.345
friends, mentors, advisors,
coaches, psychologists,

00:37:17.345 --> 00:37:20.605
people in my life, my wife, my my kids

00:37:20.605 --> 00:37:22.845
who inspire me to think
bigger about things.

00:37:22.845 --> 00:37:24.285
And I think that that is really important.

00:37:24.285 --> 00:37:27.105
You know, it, it's, it's,
it's cliche of course,

00:37:27.105 --> 00:37:28.365
but you know, that the whole,

00:37:28.365 --> 00:37:30.145
you become the PHI people
you spend most of your time

00:37:30.145 --> 00:37:31.345
with, that's a real thing.

00:37:32.445 --> 00:37:34.065
And the flip side of that is like,

00:37:34.065 --> 00:37:35.795
energy vampires are also a real thing.

00:37:35.795 --> 00:37:36.945
There are people that give you energy.

00:37:36.945 --> 00:37:38.245
They're people that take away energy.

00:37:38.245 --> 00:37:42.115
And so I've tried to be meticulous about

00:37:42.115 --> 00:37:44.225
where I'm spending my time
with whom I'm spending my time

00:37:44.225 --> 00:37:47.985
with, and do I leave those
opportunities, those engagements,

00:37:47.985 --> 00:37:51.415
those dinners, those drinks,
coffees, whatever meetings,

00:37:51.415 --> 00:37:53.645
feeling more energized and thinking bigger

00:37:53.645 --> 00:37:55.145
or less energized and thinking smaller.

00:37:56.365 --> 00:37:58.185
And it's gotten easier.

00:37:58.185 --> 00:38:01.025
I, I, you know, I, I'm incredibly
fortunate and, and, and,

00:38:01.025 --> 00:38:05.585
and I know how, how, how
lucky I am that I get

00:38:05.585 --> 00:38:07.805
to have people in my life like Seth Godin,

00:38:07.805 --> 00:38:09.085
who I can call at any time

00:38:09.085 --> 00:38:10.845
and say, Hey, here's what
I'm feeling about this.

00:38:10.845 --> 00:38:13.015
Am I looking at this?
Right? And he is like, well,

00:38:13.015 --> 00:38:15.225
like Seth's brain is like
the biggest brain ever.

00:38:15.225 --> 00:38:17.685
He is like, no, I think
bigger. Or I can call Toby

00:38:17.685 --> 00:38:19.465
or I can call, you know,
any of the people I work

00:38:19.465 --> 00:38:21.465
with at Shopify, I can be like,
Hey, like, here's what I'm,

00:38:21.465 --> 00:38:22.625
and they're like, Nope, think bigger.

00:38:24.565 --> 00:38:27.605
But it's also required that I cull and,

00:38:27.605 --> 00:38:29.365
and remove certain people from my life

00:38:29.365 --> 00:38:32.405
who make me feel smaller
and, and, and think smaller.

00:38:32.405 --> 00:38:33.945
And that, that, that's a tough thing.

00:38:33.945 --> 00:38:36.325
I mean, you know, there are
people that were in my life

00:38:36.325 --> 00:38:37.905
for a long time who just, I just,

00:38:37.905 --> 00:38:39.505
it couldn't be in my life anymore.

00:38:39.505 --> 00:38:42.505
And it's not because I outgrew
them, it's just their version

00:38:42.505 --> 00:38:44.445
of, of of their vision for their life

00:38:44.445 --> 00:38:45.445
and their, the way they look at the

00:38:45.445 --> 00:38:46.745
world's different than mine.

00:38:46.745 --> 00:38:48.775
And it is on me

00:38:48.775 --> 00:38:50.695
because I let them affect
me, but they did affect me.

00:38:50.695 --> 00:38:52.955
And so I was honest
with myself and said, I,

00:38:52.955 --> 00:38:54.115
you're living your life.

00:38:54.115 --> 00:38:56.995
I'm living mine, but every
time I spend time with you, I,

00:38:56.995 --> 00:38:59.755
my life gets adjusted just a little bit,

00:38:59.755 --> 00:39:02.035
but it gets adjusted a little
bit in the wrong direction.

00:39:02.035 --> 00:39:03.245
So therefore, every time we meet,

00:39:03.245 --> 00:39:05.815
just the volume gets turned down.

00:39:05.815 --> 00:39:09.255
I, I, I want to be, you know,
I want to be fully present.

00:39:09.255 --> 00:39:11.765
I want to be ambitious. I want live this,

00:39:11.765 --> 00:39:13.775
this life that I envision.

00:39:13.775 --> 00:39:15.925
I I've, I've tried to envision
for myself for a long time.

00:39:15.925 --> 00:39:18.955
Therefore I need people that
turn up the volume for me.

00:39:18.955 --> 00:39:21.935
And that matters, I think more
than almost anything else.

00:39:21.935 --> 00:39:23.335
I mean, you can read
great books and you can go

00:39:23.335 --> 00:39:24.895
to great conferences and
listen to great podcasts,

00:39:24.895 --> 00:39:27.455
but for me, the way that
I am able to continue

00:39:27.455 --> 00:39:32.025
to think really big about the
things is to be incredibly

00:39:32.025 --> 00:39:36.035
deliberate and disciplined about who

00:39:36.035 --> 00:39:37.765
I'm spending my time with.

00:39:37.765 --> 00:39:39.355
- Love it. Well, final question. Yeah.

00:39:39.355 --> 00:39:42.395
I'm curious who your
entrepreneur heroes are.

00:39:42.395 --> 00:39:44.595
Is it, is it your
grandfather who sold eggs?

00:39:44.595 --> 00:39:46.975
Is it Steve Jobs selling sugar water?

00:39:46.975 --> 00:39:48.435
Like who do you, who do you look to

00:39:48.435 --> 00:39:50.335
as your heroes of all time?

00:39:50.335 --> 00:39:53.615
- I mean, my grandparents and
my parents, you know, play,

00:39:53.615 --> 00:39:56.215
have played a huge role in my
life in very different ways.

00:39:58.355 --> 00:40:02.645
The person actually that I'm,
I'm, I'm the person right now

00:40:02.645 --> 00:40:05.475
that I'm, I'm, I'm really impressed with

00:40:05.475 --> 00:40:07.715
and I'm, I'm, I'm really
quite inspired by,

00:40:07.715 --> 00:40:10.455
is a guy named John Hope Bryant
actually recently joined his

00:40:10.455 --> 00:40:11.975
board from Operation Hope.

00:40:13.085 --> 00:40:15.415
John is an African American

00:40:17.255 --> 00:40:19.295
business businessman, a
business person from the us.

00:40:19.295 --> 00:40:22.295
He lives in Atlanta. I think
a couple years ago he was like

00:40:22.295 --> 00:40:25.595
awarded like Banker of the
Year, like just super, super,

00:40:25.595 --> 00:40:29.445
you know, high performing,
huge, you know, big brain,

00:40:29.445 --> 00:40:31.075
very ambitious, very successful.

00:40:31.075 --> 00:40:32.095
But a couple years ago he

00:40:32.095 --> 00:40:33.335
said, I don't want to be a banker anymore.

00:40:33.335 --> 00:40:35.175
I actually want to go
and I want to help more.

00:40:36.055 --> 00:40:37.675
I want to create more
black owned businesses.

00:40:37.675 --> 00:40:39.535
And so he just said like,
I'm going to take everything I

00:40:39.535 --> 00:40:43.225
learned as a banker with
all this great experience,

00:40:43.225 --> 00:40:45.135
all this great success,
and I'm just going to shift it

00:40:45.135 --> 00:40:46.645
over to something else.

00:40:46.645 --> 00:40:48.485
And I'm going to take that learning

00:40:48.485 --> 00:40:51.365
